nPRAGMA foreign_key_check;\n\nCOMMIT;\n\nPRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":25,"totalLines":25,"truncated":false}},"title":"workspace/in/db/rollback.sql","time":{"start":1788191736104,"end":1788191736112}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_009d720a3006014a016a95a3f7b65c87d2b551d5a96458bb65"}},"id":"prt_0588881050014TUexZV66YCfXQ","sessionID":"ses_fa7795194ffeY4beoagnDLIJ4F","messageID":"msg_058886cc1001R5yUzync3T1ZqJ"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788191736160,"sessionID":"ses_fa7795194ffeY4beoagnDLIJ4F","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_u0H1HT6Rafp9SoacAYH8aNrA","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/in/db/postcheck.sql"},"output":"<path>/workspace/in/db/postcheck.sql</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: -- Row counts should remain unchanged.\n2: SELECT 'users_row_count' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS actual_count, 6 AS expected_count\n3: FROM users;\n4: \n5: SELECT 'orders_row_count' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS actual_count, 4 AS expected_count\n6: FROM orders;\n7: \n8: -- Dependent orders for dirty users must still point at the same user ids.\n9: SELECT o.id AS order_id, o.user_id, u.email, u.status\n10: FROM orders AS o\n11: JOIN users AS u ON u.id = o.user_id\n12: WHERE o.user_id IN ('u4', 'u5', 'u6')\n13: ORDER BY o.id;\n14: \n15: -- Deterministic dirty-email cleanup results.\n16: SELECT id, email, status, created_at\n17: FROM users\n18: WHERE id IN ('u4', 'u5', 'u6')\n19: ORDER BY id;\n20: \n21: -- Constraint-oriented checks after migration.\n22: SELECT 'null_or_blank_emails' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS violation_count\n23: FROM users\n24: WHERE email IS NULL OR TRIM(email) = '';\n25: \n26: SELECT 'duplicate_emails' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS violation_count\n27: FROM (\n28:     SELECT email\n29:     FROM users\n30:     GROUP BY email\n31:     HAVING COUNT(*) > 1\n32: );\n33: \n34: SELECT 'non_active_or_null_status' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS violation_count\n35: FROM users\n36: WHERE status IS NULL OR status <> 'active';\n37: \n38: PRAGMA foreign_key_check;\n\n(End of file - total 38 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"-- Row counts should remain unchanged.\nSELECT 'users_row_count' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS actual_count, 6 AS expected_count\nFROM users;\n\nSELECT 'orders_row_count' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS actual_count, 4 AS expected_count\nFROM orders;\n\n-- Dependent orders for dirty users must still point at the same user ids.\nSELECT o.id AS order_id, o.user_id, u.email, u.status\nFROM orders AS o\nJOIN users AS u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE o.user_id IN ('u4', 'u5', 'u6')\nORDER BY o.id;\n\n-- Deterministic dirty-email cleanup results.\nSELECT id, email, status, created_at\nFROM users\nWHERE id IN ('u4', 'u5', 'u6')\nORDER BY id;\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/in/db/postcheck.sql","text":"-- Row counts should remain unchanged.\nSELECT 'users_row_count' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS actual_count, 6 AS expected_count\nFROM users;\n\nSELECT 'orders_row_count' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS actual_count, 4 AS expected_count\nFROM orders;\n\n-- Dependent orders for dirty users must still point at the same user ids.\nSELECT o.id AS order_id, o.user_id, u.email, u.status\nFROM orders AS o\nJOIN users AS u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE o.user_id IN ('u4', 'u5', 'u6')\nORDER BY o.id;\n\n-- Deterministic dirty-email cleanup results.\nSELECT id, email, status, created_at\nFROM users\nWHERE id IN ('u4', 'u5', 'u6')\nORDER BY id;\n\n-- Constraint-oriented checks after migration.\nSELECT 'null_or_blank_emails' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS violation_count\nFROM users\nWHERE email IS NULL OR TRIM(email) = '';\n\nSELECT 'duplicate_emails' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS violation_count\nFROM (\n    SELECT email\n    FROM users\n    GROUP BY email\n    HAVING COUNT(*) > 1\n);\n\nSELECT 'non_active_or_null_status' AS check_name, COUNT(*) AS violation_count\nFROM users\nWHERE status IS NULL OR status <> 'active';\n\nPRAGMA foreign_key_check;","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":38,"totalLines":38,"truncated":false}},"title":"workspace/in/db/postcheck.sql","time":{"start":1788191736151,"end":1788191736158}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_009d720a3006014a016a95a3f7b66c87d29c48ead3644cfb5a"}},"id":"prt_05888812d001udM0P7T1YHJode","sessionID":"ses_fa7795194ffeY4beoagnDLIJ4F","messageID":"msg_058886cc1001R5yUzync3T1ZqJ"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788191736177,"sessionID":"ses_fa7795194ffeY4beoagnDLIJ4F","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_70t1HD8T9zV4v3aQBTTEIDwv","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/in/db/preflight_report.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/in/db/preflight_report.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Preflight Report\n2: \n3: ## Dirty email rows before migration\n4: \n5: The existing `users` table contains three rows that would violate the target `email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE` rule:\n6: \n7: | user_id | current email | issue | migrated email |\n8: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n9: | `u4` | `ada@example.com` | duplicate of `u1` | `ada+u4@example.com` |\n10: | `u5` | `NULL` | null email | `missing+u5@example.invalid` |\n11: | `u6` | `''` | blank email | `missing+u6@example.invalid` |\n12: \n13: Per policy, `u1` remains the canonical `ada@example.com` row and is not modified.\n14: \n15: ## Dependent orders that must be preserved\n16: \n17: Dirty users have dependent order rows that must survive the migration without changing `orders.user_id`:\n18: \n19: | order_id | user_id | total_cents |\n20: | --- | --- | --- |\n21: | `o2` | `u4` | `4599` |\n22: | `o3` | `u5` | `799` |\n23: | `o4` | `u6` | `1599` |\n24: \n25: The migration must therefore preserve all user ids, preserve all order rows, and keep each order attached to the same `user_id` after email cleanup.\n\n(End of file - total 25 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Preflight Report\n\n## Dirty email rows before migration\n\nThe existing `users` table contains three rows that would violate the target `email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE` rule:\n\n| user_id | current email | issue | migrated email |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `u4` | `ada@example.com` | duplicate of `u1` | `ada+u4@example.com` |\n| `u5` | `NULL` | null email | `missing+u5@example.invalid` |\n| `u6` | `''` | blank email | `missing+u6@example.invalid` |\n\nPer policy, `u1` remains the canonical `ada@example.com` row and is not modified.\n\n## Dependent orders that must be preserved\n\nDirty users have dependent order rows that must survive the migration without changing `orders.user_id`:\n\n| order_id | user_id | total_cents |\n| --- | --- | --- |","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/in/db/preflight_report.md","text":"# Preflight Report\n\n## Dirty email rows before migration\n\nThe existing `users` table contains three rows that would violate the target `email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE` rule:\n\n| user_id | current email | issue | migrated email |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `u4` | `ada@example.com` | duplicate of `u1` | `ada+u4@example.com` |\n| `u5` | `NULL` | null email | `missing+u5@example.invalid` |\n| `u6` | `''` | blank email | `missing+u6@example.invalid` |\n\nPer policy, `u1` remains the canonical `ada@example.com` row and is not modified.\n\n## Dependent orders that must be preserved\n\nDirty users have dependent order rows that must survive the migration without changing `orders.user_id`:\n\n| order_id | user_id | total_cents |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `o2` | `u4` | `4599` |\n| `o3` | `u5` | `799` |\n| `o4` | `u6` | `1599` |\n\nThe migration must therefore preserve all user ids, preserve all order rows, and keep each order attached to the same `user_id` after email cleanup.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":25,"totalLines":25,"truncated":false}},"title":"workspace/in/db/preflight_report.md","time":{"start":1788191736162,"end":1788191736176}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_009d720a3006014a016a95a3f7b67487d28245c71c7b131a17"}},"id":"prt_05888815f0016JMozgHoXczyKF","sessionID":"ses_fa7795194ffeY4beoagnDLIJ4F","messageID":"msg_058886cc1001R5yUzync3T1ZqJ"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788191736179,"sessionID":"ses_fa7795194ffeY4beoagnDLIJ4F","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_6yyN3PmaatUqNVsmD3yJfBzh","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/in/db/migration_report.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/in/db/migration_report.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Migration Report\n2: \n3: ## Strategy\n4: \n5: The migration rebuilds `users` inside an explicit transaction instead of mutating rows in place or dropping user data. It:\n6: \n7: 1. disables foreign-key enforcement temporarily,\n8: 2. creates a replacement `users` table with `email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE` and `status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'`,\n9: 3. copies every existing user row into the new table while preserving `id`, `name`, and historical `created_at`,\n10: 4. applies deterministic cleanup for known dirty emails,\n11: 5. swaps the rebuilt table into place, and\n12: 6. runs `PRAGMA foreign_key_check` before commit.\n13: \n14: Because user ids are preserved exactly, dependent `orders.user_id` references remain attached to the same users.\n15: \n16: ## Dirty-data cleanup\n17: \n18: The migration applies the required email fixes exactly:\n19: \n20: | user_id | old email | new email |\n21: | --- | --- | --- |\n22: | `u4` | `ada@example.com` | `ada+u4@example.com` |\n23: | `u5` | `NULL` | `missing+u5@example.invalid` |\n24: | `u6` | `''` | `missing+u6@example.invalid` |\n25: \n26: `u1` keeps `ada@example.com` unchanged so the original canonical address is preserved.\n27: \n28: ## Idempotency approach\n29: \n30: The script is written as a full table rebuild from the current `users` contents. Re-running it does not duplicate rows and re-applies the same deterministic cleanup values for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`, so the resulting dataset remains stable.\n31: \n32: ## Rollback behavior and limitation\n33: \n34: `rollback.sql` restores the pre-migration `users` schema shape to:\n35: \n36: - `id`\n37: - `email`\n38: - `name`\n39: - `created_at`\n40: \n41: It preserves all user rows and all order rows, including orders for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`, because ids are not changed. The rollback restores the old schema shape, not the old dirty email values.\n42: \n43: ## Post-migration verification\n44: \n45: Run `postcheck.sql` after the migration. It verifies:\n46: \n47: - user and order row counts,\n48: - preserved dependent orders for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`,\n49: - exact cleaned email values for dirty users,\n50: - absence of null or blank emails,\n51: - absence of duplicate emails,\n52: - non-null active status values, and\n53: - foreign-key integrity via `PRAGMA foreign_key_check`.\n\n(End of file - total 53 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Migration Report\n\n## Strategy\n\nThe migration rebuilds `users` inside an explicit transaction instead of mutating rows in place or dropping user data. It:\n\n1. disables foreign-key enforcement temporarily,\n2. creates a replacement `users` table with `email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE` and `status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'`,\n3. copies every existing user row into the new table while preserving `id`, `name`, and historical `created_at`,\n4. applies deterministic cleanup for known dirty emails,\n5. swaps the rebuilt table into place, and\n6. runs `PRAGMA foreign_key_check` before commit.\n\nBecause user ids are preserved exactly, dependent `orders.user_id` references remain attached to the same users.\n\n## Dirty-data cleanup\n\nThe migration applies the required email fixes exactly:\n\n| user_id | old email | new email |","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/in/db/migration_report.md","text":"# Migration Report\n\n## Strategy\n\nThe migration rebuilds `users` inside an explicit transaction instead of mutating rows in place or dropping user data. It:\n\n1. disables foreign-key enforcement temporarily,\n2. creates a replacement `users` table with `email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE` and `status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'`,\n3. copies every existing user row into the new table while preserving `id`, `name`, and historical `created_at`,\n4. applies deterministic cleanup for known dirty emails,\n5. swaps the rebuilt table into place, and\n6. runs `PRAGMA foreign_key_check` before commit.\n\nBecause user ids are preserved exactly, dependent `orders.user_id` references remain attached to the same users.\n\n## Dirty-data cleanup\n\nThe migration applies the required email fixes exactly:\n\n| user_id | old email | new email |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `u4` | `ada@example.com` | `ada+u4@example.com` |\n| `u5` | `NULL` | `missing+u5@example.invalid` |\n| `u6` | `''` | `missing+u6@example.invalid` |\n\n`u1` keeps `ada@example.com` unchanged so the original canonical address is preserved.\n\n## Idempotency approach\n\nThe script is written as a full table rebuild from the current `users` contents. Re-running it does not duplicate rows and re-applies the same deterministic cleanup values for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`, so the resulting dataset remains stable.\n\n## Rollback behavior and limitation\n\n`rollback.sql` restores the pre-migration `users` schema shape to:\n\n- `id`\n- `email`\n- `name`\n- `created_at`\n\nIt preserves all user rows and all order rows, including orders for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`, because ids are not changed. The rollback restores the old schema shape, not the old dirty email values.\n\n## Post-migration verification\n\nRun `postcheck.sql` after the migration. It verifies:\n\n- user and order row counts,\n- preserved dependent orders for `u4`, `u5`, and `u6`,\n- exact cleaned email values for dirty users,\n- absence of null or blank emails,\n- absence of duplicate emails,\n- non-null active status values, and\n- foreign-key integrity via `PRAGMA foreign_key_check`.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":53,"totalLines":53,"truncated":false}},"title":"workspace/in/db/migration_report.md","time":{"start":1788191736171,"end":1788191736178}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_009d720a3006014a016a95a3f7b67c87d2ba7dfc14229537ca"}},"id":"prt_058888168001c8ho39bpFSBIlu","sessionID":"ses_fa7795194ffeY4beoagnDLIJ4F","messageID":"msg_058886cc1001R5yUzync3T1ZqJ"}}
